\title[Five Fields, One Law]{Five Fields, One Law: An Exact Information-Theoretic Law
for Splitting Types at the Conductor, with the Cubic $x^3-4x+1$ of Discriminant $229$}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{October 2026}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
For a prime $p$ and a monic integer cubic $f$, the \emph{splitting type} of $p$ records how
$f$ factors modulo $p$. Experiments on five independent $S_3$ cubic fields found that the
residue class of $p$ modulo the conductor $D$ of the quadratic resolvent carries, as measured
by Shannon mutual information, about one bit about the splitting type. The most recent of
these fields comes from $f=x^3-4x+1$, with $D=229$, where the plug-in estimate was $1.0078$
bits. This paper explains that observation with an exact theorem. In the
\emph{Chebotarev fibre-product model}, a Frobenius element uniform on a finite group $G$ and
a residue class uniform on a finite group $R$ are coupled only through a common quotient
$\Delta$ with balanced fibres. In this model, any read-out of the Frobenius element that
determines its image in $\Delta$ has mutual information with the residue class equal to
exactly $\log_2|\Delta|$. For every symmetric group $S_n$ with $n\ge2$, this value is
exactly one bit. For $S_3$ the bit splits as $H(T)=\tfrac23+\tfrac12\log_23$ and
$H(T\mid\text{class})=\tfrac12\log_23-\tfrac13$. On the arithmetic side we prove, by
elementary means, that for every prime $p\notin\{2,229\}$ the cubic $x^3-4x+1$ has exactly
one root modulo $p$ if and only if $\leg{p}{229}=-1$, and that its root count is never $2$.
The proof is an explicit Frobenius computation followed by quadratic reciprocity. We also
prove that $f$ is irreducible over $\Q$, that its discriminant is not a rational square, and
that it has three real roots, so $f$ defines a totally real $S_3$ cubic field. Together these
results identify the ``five fields'' phenomenon as five instances of one formula, and show
that the observed excess above one bit is finite-sample bias, not signal.
\end{abstract}

\maketitle

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The observation}
Let $f(x)=x^3-4x+1$. For each prime $p$ let $T(p)\in\{0,1,3\}$ be the number of roots of $f$
in $\F_p$; we will show that the value $2$ never occurs for $p\ne229$. Write $c(p)=p\bmod229$
for the residue class of $p$ modulo the discriminant
\[
\operatorname{disc}(f)=-4(-4)^3-27\cdot1^2=229,
\]
which is prime. An empirical study computed the plug-in mutual information $\hat I(c;T)$
over an initial segment of primes and found $\hat I\approx1.0078$ bits. This was very far
from the value $0$ expected if $c$ and $T$ were independent; a permutation test gave
$z\approx+263$. Four earlier, independent $S_3$ cubics with different discriminants had given
values equally close to $1$. This paper answers three questions: why exactly one bit, why for
every field, and what the extra $0.0078$ is.

\subsection{Summary of results}
\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Fibre-Product Law} (Theorem~\ref{thm:fibre}). In a uniform fibre product
$G\times_\Delta R$ with balanced fibres, any read-out of the $G$-coordinate that determines
its image in $\Delta$ has mutual information with the $R$-coordinate exactly
$\log_2|\Delta|$.
\item \textbf{Universal Symmetric-Group Law} (Theorem~\ref{thm:Sn}). For every $n\ge2$ the
cycle-type channel of $S_n\times_{\{\pm1\}}R$ carries exactly one bit. For $S_3$ the
root-count channel carries one bit (Theorem~\ref{thm:S3}), with entropy split given in
Proposition~\ref{prop:split}. This holds for every odd prime conductor
(Theorem~\ref{thm:cond}), in particular $229$.
\item \textbf{Splitting-Type Law for $x^3-4x+1$} (Theorems~\ref{thm:split-disc}
and~\ref{thm:split-cond}). For primes $p\notin\{2,229\}$, $f$ has exactly one root mod $p$
$\iff$ $229$ is a non-square mod $p$ $\iff$ $\leg p{229}=-1$.
\item \textbf{Fine structure} (Propositions~\ref{prop:no2}--\ref{prop:witness}). The root
count is never $2$. On the residue side the type is $1+1+1$ or $3$. The primes $3$ and $461$
lie in the same class mod $229$ but have different types.
\item \textbf{The field} (Proposition~\ref{prop:field}). $f$ is irreducible over $\Q$, $229$
is not a rational square, and $f$ has three real roots.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Definitions}

Throughout, $\log_2$ is the base-$2$ logarithm, and all probability spaces are finite sets
carrying the uniform distribution.

\begin{definition}[Counting entropy]
Let $s$ be a finite nonempty set and $X\colon s\to\mathcal X$. With
$n_x=\#\{\omega\in s:X(\omega)=x\}$ and $N=\#s$,
\[
H_s(X)=\sum_x\frac{n_x}{N}\log_2\frac{N}{n_x}=\log_2N-\frac1N\sum_xn_x\log_2n_x .
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Conditional entropy, mutual information]
For $X\colon s\to\mathcal X$ and $Y\colon s\to\mathcal Y$, set
$H_s(X\mid Y)=H_s(X,Y)-H_s(Y)$ and
\[
I_s(X;Y)=H_s(X)-H_s(X\mid Y)=\sum_{x,y}\frac{n_{x,y}}{N}\log_2\frac{N\,n_{x,y}}{n_x\,m_y},
\]
where $n_{x,y}=\#\{X=x,\,Y=y\}$ and $m_y=\#\{Y=y\}$, and terms with $n_{x,y}=0$ are omitted.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Fibre product]
Let $G,R$ be finite sets and $\varepsilon\colon G\to\Delta$, $\chi\colon R\to\Delta$. The
fibre product is $G\times_\Delta R=\{(\sigma,r):\varepsilon(\sigma)=\chi(r)\}$. The map
$\varepsilon$ is \emph{balanced with fibre size $A>0$} if $\#\varepsilon^{-1}(d)=A$ for all
$d\in\Delta$, and similarly for $\chi$ with fibre size $B>0$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}
A map $\tau\colon G\to\beta$ \emph{determines} $\varepsilon$ if $\varepsilon=e\circ\tau$ for
some $e\colon\beta\to\Delta$.
\end{definition}

\begin{remark}[The Chebotarev fibre-product model]
Let $K/\Q$ be Galois with group $G$, and suppose an abelian subextension with group $\Delta$
lies in $\Q(\zeta_D)$. Then the restriction of $\Frob_p$ to it is $\chi(p\bmod D)$ for the
Artin map $\chi\colon(\Z/D)^\times\to\Delta$. Chebotarev's density theorem together with
Dirichlet's theorem gives asymptotic equidistribution of $(\Frob_p,\,p\bmod D)$ on
$G\times_\Delta(\Z/D)^\times$. For an $S_3$ cubic field whose quadratic resolvent has prime
conductor $\ell$, $\Delta=\{\pm1\}$, $\varepsilon=\sgn$ and $\chi=\leg{\cdot}{\ell}$.
Sections~\ref{sec:law}--\ref{sec:sym} are statements about this finite model;
Section~\ref{sec:cubic} establishes the arithmetic coupling for $x^3-4x+1$.
\end{remark}

\section{The Fibre-Product Law}\label{sec:law}

\begin{theorem}[Fibre-Product Law]\label{thm:fibre}
Let $\varepsilon\colon G\to\Delta$ and $\chi\colon R\to\Delta$ be balanced with fibre sizes
$A,B>0$, with $\Delta$ finite, and let $\tau\colon G\to\beta$ determine $\varepsilon$. On the
uniform fibre product $s=G\times_\Delta R$,
\[
I_s\bigl(\tau(\sigma);\,r\bigr)=\log_2|\Delta| .
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
If $\Delta=\varnothing$ both sides vanish. Otherwise write $a_v=\#\tau^{-1}(v)$ and count:
$N=\#s=|G|B=|\Delta|AB$; $M_c=\#\varepsilon^{-1}(\chi(c))=A$; $m_v=a_vB$; and
$n_{c,v}=\#\{\sigma:\varepsilon(\sigma)=\chi(c),\,\tau(\sigma)=v\}$. If $n_{c,v}\ne0$, choose
$\sigma_0$ in this set. Then $e(v)=\varepsilon(\sigma_0)=\chi(c)$, so every $\sigma$ with
$\tau(\sigma)=v$ satisfies $\varepsilon(\sigma)=\chi(c)$, and $n_{c,v}=a_v$. Every nonzero
term therefore has
\[
\log_2\frac{N\,n_{c,v}}{m_v\,M_c}=\log_2\frac{|\Delta|AB\,a_v}{a_vB\,A}=\log_2|\Delta|,
\]
and since $\sum n_{c,v}=N$ the sum equals $\log_2|\Delta|$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Marginal invariance]\label{prop:marg}
If $\chi$ is balanced with fibre size $B>0$, then for every $\tau$ the entropy of $\tau(\sigma)$
on $G\times_\Delta R$ equals $H_G(\tau)$ under the uniform law on $G$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$m_v/N=a_vB/(|G|B)=a_v/|G|$, and every $\sigma$ has a partner since the fibres of $\chi$ are
nonempty.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Group form]\label{cor:group}
If $\varepsilon\colon G\to H$ and $\chi\colon R\to H$ are surjective homomorphisms of finite
groups and $\tau$ determines $\varepsilon$, then $I(\tau;r)=\log_2|H|$ on $G\times_HR$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
The fibres of a surjective homomorphism are cosets of its kernel, all of the same positive
size. Apply Theorem~\ref{thm:fibre}.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Trivial quotient]
If $H$ is trivial, the channel carries $0$ bits.
\end{corollary}

\section{Symmetric Groups and Prime Conductors}\label{sec:sym}

For $\sigma\in S_n$ of cycle type $\lambda$ with $k$ parts, $\sgn\sigma=(-1)^{n+k}$, so the
cycle type determines the sign.

\begin{theorem}[Universal Symmetric-Group Law]\label{thm:Sn}
Let $n\ge2$ and let $R$ be a finite group with a surjective homomorphism
$\chi\colon R\to\{\pm1\}$. On $\{(\sigma,r)\in S_n\times R:\sgn\sigma=\chi(r)\}$, the mutual
information between the cycle type of $\sigma$ and $r$ is exactly $1$ bit.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$\sgn$ is surjective for $n\ge2$ and is determined by the cycle type; apply
Corollary~\ref{cor:group} with $|H|=2$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:rho}
For $\sigma\in S_3$ let $\fix(\sigma)$ be the number of fixed points, and let $\rho(1)=-1$,
$\rho(k)=+1$ for $k\ne1$. Then $\rho(\fix\sigma)=\sgn\sigma$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Identity: $3$ fixed points, sign $+1$. Transpositions: $1$ fixed point, sign $-1$.
$3$-cycles: $0$ fixed points, sign $+1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[$S_3$ root-count law]\label{thm:S3}
For every finite group $R$ with a surjective $\chi\colon R\to\{\pm1\}$, the root-count channel
of $S_3\times_{\{\pm1\}}R$ carries exactly $1$ bit.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proposition}[Entropy split]\label{prop:split}
Under the uniform law on $S_3$, $T=\fix(\sigma)$ takes the values $3,1,0$ with probabilities
$\tfrac16,\tfrac12,\tfrac13$, so
\[
H(T)=\tfrac16\log_26+\tfrac12\log_22+\tfrac13\log_23=\tfrac23+\tfrac12\log_23\approx1.4591,
\]
and on any fibre product as in Theorem~\ref{thm:S3},
\[
H(T\mid r)=\tfrac12\log_23-\tfrac13\approx0.4591 .
\]
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Use $\log_26=1+\log_23$, Proposition~\ref{prop:marg} and Theorem~\ref{thm:S3}.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
On the non-residue half the type is certain. On the residue half it is ``three roots'' or
``no root'' with probabilities $\tfrac13,\tfrac23$, giving $h(\tfrac13)=\log_23-\tfrac23$.
Averaging gives $\tfrac12h(\tfrac13)$. The bit is exactly the sign bit.
\end{remark}

\begin{theorem}[Prime-conductor form]\label{thm:cond}
For every odd prime $\ell$, on $S_3\times_{\{\pm1\}}(\Z/\ell)^\times$ coupled by
$\sgn\sigma=\leg r\ell$, the root-count channel carries exactly $1$ bit; in particular for
$\ell=229$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The quadratic character of $\F_\ell^\times$ is surjective because $\F_\ell$ has a non-square
for odd $\ell$; apply Theorem~\ref{thm:S3}.
\end{proof}

\section{The Cubic $x^3-4x+1$}\label{sec:cubic}

Let $f(x)=x^3-4x+1$ over a commutative ring.

\subsection{The discriminant identity}

\begin{lemma}[Two-root relations]\label{lem:rel}
In an integral domain, if $r\ne s$ are roots of $f$, then $r^2+rs+s^2=4$ and $rs(r+s)=1$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
$f(r)-f(s)=(r-s)(r^2+rs+s^2-4)$; cancel $r-s$. Then
$rs(r+s)=r(r^2+rs+s^2)-r^3=4r-r^3=1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Third root]\label{lem:third}
Under the same hypotheses $-(r+s)$ is a root.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
$f(-(r+s))=-(r+s)(r^2+rs+s^2-4)-(rs(r+s)-1)=0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Discriminant $229$]\label{thm:disc}
In an integral domain, if $r\ne s$ are roots of $f$ and $\delta=(r-s)(r+2s)(2r+s)$, then
$\delta^2=229$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The ring identity $\delta^2=4(r^2+rs+s^2)^3-27(rs(r+s))^2$ and Lemma~\ref{lem:rel} give
$4\cdot64-27=229$.
\end{proof}

Up to sign, $\delta$ is the Vandermonde product of the roots $r,s,t=-(r+s)$, since
$r-t=2r+s$ and $s-t=r+2s$.

\begin{corollary}\label{cor:unique}
If $229$ is not a square in an integral domain $K$, then $f$ has at most one root in $K$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{lemma}[Residual discriminant]\label{lem:resid}
If $f(r)=0$ then $(16-3r^2)(3r^2-4)^2=229$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
The difference of the two sides is $(-27r^3+108r+27)f(r)$.
\end{proof}

Here $16-3r^2=\operatorname{disc}\bigl(x^2+rx+r^2-4\bigr)$ and $3r^2-4=f'(r)$.

\subsection{Frobenius and Stickelberger}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:fixfrob}
If $L\supseteq\F_p$ is a field and $x\in L$ satisfies $x^p=x$, then $x\in\F_p$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
The $p$ elements of $\F_p$ already account for all roots of the degree-$p$ polynomial $X^p-X$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Stickelberger for $f$]\label{thm:stick}
If $f$ has no root in $\F_p$, then $229$ is a square in $\F_p$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$f$ is irreducible, so $L=\F_p[x]/(f)$ is a field with a root $\alpha$. Frobenius
$y\mapsto y^p$ permutes the roots of $f$ in $L$ and, by Lemma~\ref{lem:fixfrob}, fixes none.
Put $\beta=\alpha^p\ne\alpha$ and $\theta=-(\alpha+\beta)$. Every root lies in
$\{\alpha,\beta,\theta\}$, since a root $y\ne\alpha$ satisfies
$(y-\beta)(y-\theta)=0$ by Lemma~\ref{lem:rel}. The root $\beta^p$ is not $\beta$, and not
$\alpha$ (else $\theta^p=-(\beta+\alpha)=\theta$). Hence $\beta^p=\theta$ and Frobenius is the
$3$-cycle $\alpha\mapsto\beta\mapsto\theta$. Then
\[
\delta^p=(\beta-\theta)(\beta+2\theta)(2\beta+\theta)
=(\alpha+2\beta)\bigl(-(2\alpha+\beta)\bigr)(\beta-\alpha)=\delta,
\]
so $\delta\in\F_p$ and $\delta^2=229$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:disc}.
\end{proof}

\subsection{One root plus a square discriminant}

\begin{lemma}[Second root]\label{lem:second}
Let $K$ be a field with $2\ne0\ne229$. If $f(r)=0$ and $229$ is a square in $K$, then $f$ has a
root $s\ne r$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Write $229=d^2$. By Lemma~\ref{lem:resid}, $w=3r^2-4\ne0$; set $e=d/w$, so
$e^2=16-3r^2\ne0$. If $(2s+r)^2=e^2$ then $s^2+rs+r^2-4=0$ and
$f(s)=(s-r)(s^2+rs+r^2-4)=0$. The two values $(-r\pm e)/2$ are distinct, so one of them
differs from $r$.
\end{proof}

\subsection{The splitting-type law}

\begin{theorem}[Splitting-type law, discriminant form]\label{thm:split-disc}
For primes $p\notin\{2,229\}$, $f$ has exactly one root in $\F_p$ if and only if $229$ is a
non-square in $\F_p$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
($\Rightarrow$) Lemma~\ref{lem:second}. ($\Leftarrow$) Theorem~\ref{thm:stick} gives a root
and Corollary~\ref{cor:unique} gives uniqueness.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Splitting-type law, conductor form]\label{thm:split-cond}
For primes $p\notin\{2,229\}$, $f$ has exactly one root modulo $p$ if and only if $p$ is a
quadratic non-residue modulo $229$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Since $229\equiv1\pmod4$, quadratic reciprocity gives $\leg{229}p=\leg p{229}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Type--sign coupling]\label{cor:coupling}
For primes $p\notin\{2,229\}$, $\rho(T(p))=\leg p{229}$, where $\rho$ is as in
Lemma~\ref{lem:rho}. So the sign of $\Frob_p$ is the Legendre symbol modulo $229$, which is
exactly the coupling of the fibre-product model.
\end{corollary}

\subsection{Fine structure}

\begin{proposition}[No type with two roots]\label{prop:no2}
In an integral domain with $229\ne0$, if $r\ne s$ are roots then $-(r+s)\notin\{r,s\}$.
Hence for $p\ne229$ the root count mod $p$ is never $2$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
$-(r+s)=r$ forces $2r+s=0$ and so $\delta=0$, contradicting $\delta^2=229\ne0$; similarly
for $s$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Residue branch]
If $p\notin\{2,229\}$, $229$ is a square mod $p$, and $f$ has a root mod $p$, then $f$ has three
distinct roots mod $p$. So on the residue side the type is $1+1+1$ or $3$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proposition}[Same class, different type]\label{prop:witness}
$461\equiv3\pmod{229}$. The cubic $f$ has no root mod $3$, since $f(0)\equiv f(1)\equiv f(2)\equiv1$,
while mod $461$ it has the three distinct roots $162,368,392$.
\end{proposition}

\subsection{The field over $\Q$ and $\R$}

\begin{proposition}\label{prop:field}
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] $f$ has no rational root and is irreducible over $\Q$.
\item[(b)] $229$ is not a rational square, so $\operatorname{Gal}(f/\Q)\cong S_3$.
\item[(c)] $f$ has real roots in $(-3,-2)$, $(0,1)$ and $(1,2)$, so the cubic field is totally
real.
\end{enumerate}
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
(a) A root $a/b$ in lowest terms gives $a^3-4ab^2+b^3=0$; checking the nine pairs modulo $3$
forces $3\mid a$ and $3\mid b$. (b) $15^2<229<16^2$. (c) $f(-3)=-14$, $f(-2)=1$, $f(0)=1$,
$f(1)=-2$, $f(2)=1$, and apply the intermediate value theorem.
\end{proof}

\section{Synthesis}
Proposition~\ref{prop:field} makes the splitting field of $f$ an $S_3$ extension with
quadratic subfield $\Q(\sqrt{229})$ of prime conductor $229$. Corollary~\ref{cor:coupling}
identifies the coupling map with $\leg{\cdot}{229}$. Theorem~\ref{thm:cond} gives exactly $1$
bit, and Proposition~\ref{prop:split} shows how the bit is distributed. The final value
involves neither $229$ nor the cubic; only $|\Delta|=2$ enters. Five fields, five
discriminants, one formula: $I=\log_2|G^{\mathrm{ab}}|=\log_22$.

\section{Algorithms}
\textbf{A (root count via Frobenius gcd).} Compute $x^p\bmod f$ in $\F_p[x]/(f)$ by repeated
squaring with $x^3=4x-1$. The root count is $\deg\gcd(x^p-x,f)$. Cost $O(\log p)$.

\textbf{B (plug-in mutual information).} $\hat I=H(\hat c)+H(\hat t)-H(\hat c,\hat t)$ from
the contingency counts; $O(N)$.

\textbf{C (exact fibre-product channel).} Enumerate $S_n\times(\Z/\ell)^\times$, keep the
pairs with $\sgn\sigma=\leg r\ell$, and apply B. The output is exactly $1$ whenever the
read-out determines the sign.

\textbf{D (Legendre prediction).} $T(p)=1\iff p^{114}\equiv-1\pmod{229}$.

\section{Numerical Illustrations}
These computations illustrate the theorems; they are not part of the proofs. For all $17{,}982$
primes $3\le p\le200{,}000$ with $p\ne229$, the root count is $1$ exactly when
$\leg p{229}=-1$, and the value $2$ never occurs.

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{cccc}
\toprule
roots & count & frequency & Chebotarev density\\
\midrule
$0$ & $6008$ & $0.3341$ & $1/3$\\
$1$ & $8987$ & $0.4998$ & $1/2$\\
$2$ & $0$ & $0$ & $0$\\
$3$ & $2987$ & $0.1661$ & $1/6$\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

Algorithm C returns $I=1$ to within floating-point rounding for cycle types on
$S_2,\dots,S_5$ with $\ell\in\{5,7,13\}$, and for root counts on $S_3$ with $\ell=229$. The
fixed-point count on $S_4$, which does not determine the sign, gives $I\approx0.6556$.

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{rcc}
\toprule
$N$ & $\hat I_N$ & excess\\
\midrule
$1{,}000$ & $1.0737$ & $+0.0737$\\
$2{,}000$ & $1.0320$ & $+0.0320$\\
$5{,}000$ & $1.0106$ & $+0.0106$\\
$10{,}000$ & $1.0045$ & $+0.0045$\\
$17{,}982$ & $1.0023$ & $+0.0023$\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

The excess decreases roughly in proportion to $1/N$, consistent with the Miller--Madow bias of
plug-in estimators. Since the true value is exactly $1$, the excess is bias, not signal.

\section{Discussion}
Every nonzero cell of the contingency table contributes the same log-ratio $\log_2|\Delta|$.
Information about the residue class reaches the read-out only through the abelian quotient,
which is the information-theoretic face of the fact that congruence conditions see only the
abelianisation. The hypothesis that $\tau$ determines $\varepsilon$ matters: in the fibre
product, $\tau$ and $r$ are conditionally independent given the common value
$\varepsilon(\sigma)=\chi(r)$. This suggests $I(\tau;r)=I_G(\tau;\varepsilon)$ in general,
with Theorem~\ref{thm:fibre} as the case where this equals $\log_2|\Delta|$.
The results of Sections~\ref{sec:law}--\ref{sec:sym} are exact statements about the
equidistributed model. Chebotarev's theorem connects primes to the model; the coupling for
$x^3-4x+1$ is proved unconditionally in Section~\ref{sec:cubic}.

\section{Future Directions}
\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Abelianisation--projection law.} Conjecture:
$I(p\bmod f;\tau)=I_G(\tau;\mathrm{ab})$ for every Galois field. For example, $D_4$ quartics
would give $2$ bits and $A_4$ fields $\log_23$ bits.
\item \textbf{Bias law.} Determine the first-order term of $\mathbb E[\hat I_N]-1$ from the
occupied-cell structure of the contingency table, and recalibrate reported $z$-scores.
\item \textbf{Higher degree.} Prove the analogue of Corollary~\ref{cor:coupling} for explicit
$S_4$ and $S_5$ fields.
\item \textbf{Composite conductors.} Extend Theorem~\ref{thm:cond} to quadratic resolvents with
composite or even conductor.
\end{enumerate}

